CHAPTER II
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN
Article 4
Women and children victims of acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor shall receive the following rights:
a. Right to guarantee space for community participation in government, including in decision-making/policy-making;
b. Right to population documents and civil registration;
c. Right to Legal Aid;
d. Right to be respected for their dignity and honor as human beings; e. Right to health and psychological recovery from suffering experienced by victims;
f. Right to determine their own decisions;
g. Right to obtain information;
h. Right to confidentiality;
i. Right to compensation;
j. Right to social rehabilitation;
k. Right to complaint handling;
- 9 -
l. Right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and
m. Right to assistance.

Article 10
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of Customary Institutions in implementing protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:
a. customary institutions have the responsibility to safeguard the dignity and human rights of women and children in accordance with highly upheld humanitarian values;
b. being responsible for preventing all forms of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children within the customary environment;
c. ensuring gender equality and children's rights are respected, without discrimination based on gender or age;
d. being responsible for raising awareness among customary communities regarding the rights of women and children, including awareness of the importance of protection and respect for their rights;
e. conducting outreach programs and training for members of customary communities, especially customary leaders, about the rights of women and children and effective protection methods;
f. providing psychosocial and legal support to victims of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children including facilitating victims' access to protection and legal institutions;
g. playing an active role in the reporting process and law enforcement regarding cases of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment - 12 - and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children in customary communities;
h. facilitating the establishment of PPA institutions at the customary level tasked with protecting their rights and providing assistance and support to victims;
i. integrating customary values that support equality and PPA into community outreach and education programs; and j. building partnerships with non-governmental organizations, protection institutions, and educational institutions to strengthen PPA efforts. (2) The obligations and responsibilities of Business Entities in implementing protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:
a. being obligated to comply with all laws, regulations, and rules related to PPA at the national and regional levels; b. providing a safe and supportive work environment, free from harassment, discrimination, violence, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children;
c. providing shelters for female workers at the workplace; d. forming partnerships with regional governments in PPA; e. providing empowerment for women and children as a shared responsibility with regional governments;
f. conducting outreach programs in communities surrounding the company to raise awareness about PPA;
g. creating policies, programs, and PPA activities;
h. supporting social programs that enhance women's economic independence and support children's education; i. supporting the establishment of protection centers and services for victims of violence against women and children; and
j. supporting PPA programs in Pulang Pisau Regency through Corporate Social Responsibility instruments and/or assistance in other forms in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.
